Developer note. The dialogs nwergm.dlg, nwergm_simulate.dlg and nwergm_estat.dlg are generated: edit dlg/nwergm_dialog_spec.py (one entry per term or option), run
python3 dlg/gen_dialogs.py to rewrite the three .dlg files, then python3 dlg/check_dialogs.py (static checks and coverage of the syntax lines and of docs/ERGM_TERMS_INVENTORY.csv).
dlg/smoke_dialogs.py builds the command of every entry as the dialog would and runs it in Stata (-e); see its docstring. Do not edit the .dlg files by hand.
check_dialogs.py also runs `db <dialog>` in Stata -e for each dialog (needs --work <scratch dir> and --nwcore <core dir>); rc must be 0. Stata rejects a dialog PROGRAM with too many statements (rc 1001), so long blocks are split into sub-programs; every dialog needs SYNCHRONOUS_ONLY, otherwise `db` waits for the window.
Layout rule for all dialogs (also for the nwsaom dialogs): at most 9 tabs, tab titles of at most 10 characters, at least 90 px of dialog width per tab (Stata draws one row of tabs and cuts longer titles). If more areas are needed, put several sections in one tab and choose between them with a drop-down ("Section:") that shows/hides their controls (see assemble() in gen_dialogs.py; the show/hide scripts are split into sub-scripts of 40 lines). check_dialogs.py enforces the rule.

Command-assembly test (run before every release, with the other checks): python3 dlg/cmd_assembly_test.py --nwcore <core dir>. It runs the PROGRAM command of every dialog in the real dialog engine (Stata -e) and compares the command it builds; the engine silently aborts a program whose `if` condition it cannot evaluate (parentheses!), and then OK does nothing, which check_dialogs.py cannot see.
